

SECTION 1 — Executive Summary

Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE): Proton‑Laser Interaction & Cosmic Data Integration FrameworkAuthor: C. Matthew Blackwell

The Astrometric Simulation Engine (ASE) is a multi‑scale computational architecture designed to unify astrophysical environmental datasets with high‑energy proton–laser interaction physics inside a secure, HPC‑accelerated simulation framework. ASE addresses a longstanding gap in computational astrophysics: the absence of platforms capable of integrating observational cosmic data with microphysical plasma‑laser solvers. By merging cosmic‑ray spectra, star‑formation models, interstellar medium (ISM) density maps, and GEANT4 particle‑interaction datasets with Particle‑in‑Cell (PIC) and Vlasov–Maxwell solvers, ASE enables proton‑laser simulations to be conducted under astrophysically realistic conditions.

The system incorporates a hybrid solver architecture, GPU‑accelerated computation, distributed HPC scheduling, and a zero‑trust cryptographic security kernel. A Qt/Python‑based Simulation Designer GUI provides interactive configuration, 3D visualization, and comparative analysis across cosmic and plasma domains. Together, these components establish ASE as a research‑grade platform capable of modeling proton acceleration mechanisms—including Target Normal Sheath Acceleration (TNSA) and Radiation Pressure Acceleration (RPA)—within astrophysical magnetic structures.

ASE’s unified architecture supports investigations in cosmic‑ray acceleration, plasma astrophysics, laser‑driven proton dynamics, and high‑energy particle transport across interstellar environments. It represents a novel computational foundation for cross‑disciplinary research at the intersection of astrophysics, plasma physics, and high‑energy particle science.

